# 016 — Storage drivers for the VFS: ATA, AHCI and USB mass storage — done

- Type: feature · Priority: medium · Status: done · Date: 2026-10-03

## Problem
`vfs_server` itself worked with the primary ATA channel (PIO). When booting from a USB image and on most real machines (AHCI), there was no disk.

## Done
- Block access was moved into the ring 3 services `ata`, `ahci`, `usb_storage` with a common `BLOCK_INFO/ATTACH/READ` protocol (`mind::block`); `vfs_server` connects to them over IPC and mounts the first FAT volume.
- Kernel: a PCI scanner with BAR sizes, an MMIO capability (uncached mapping), driver DMA regions with 64 KiB alignment; `ahci`/`usb_storage` are started only when a controller is present.
- `usb_storage`: xHCI (handoff from the firmware, reset, command/event/transfer rings, USB 2/3 ports), device addressing, Bulk-Only Transport, SCSI TEST UNIT READY/READ CAPACITY/READ(10).
- `ahci`: BIOS/OS handoff, the first SATA port, IDENTIFY, READ DMA EXT.

## Verification
- `tests/usb_image_smoke.py`: `RUN files` reads the USB image via xHCI (acceptance criterion).
- `tests/qemu_smoke.py --suites ahci`: disk on an AHCI controller; `services`: the path via `ata`.
- Manually: a USB 2.0 port with reset and a USB keyboard in front of the drive (`qemu-xhci,p3=0`).

## Limitations
The drivers poll the controllers without interrupts; no NVMe; 512-byte sectors only; a single drive is mounted as the root.
